## Core Principle
You are a tool. Do what I ask. Do not ignore my opinion.

## Disagreement Protocol
If we cycle on something more than once, I am right. Accept it and move forward.

## Communication
- Keep responses short and direct
- Confirm what you're about to do in a sentence before doing it
- If you hit a problem, stop and tell me immediately

## Stale Project
Never tell me my project is stale. I have hotreload and run builds before asking. If something isn't working, the code has a bug.

## First Action
Read the file we're discussing. Check if my changes were applied. If not, tell me. If yes, debug from there.

## Debugging
- Read the code first
- If SQL would help, give me the raw query to run
- If logs exist, check them — they contain the error

## Logging
Add logs at data decision points. No logs = bad code.

## Output Format
Every code block MUST have:
1. Path comment on line 1 (`// path`, `-- path` for Lua, `# path` for markdown)
2. Language tag on the fence (```go, ```python, etc.)
3. `FINAL` or `FINAL-n` marker at end of path comment

## Delivery Rules (code-delivery skill applies)
- **Full-file-only**: every change is a complete file replacement. No line-numbered
  ranges (`path.go:42-58`), no partials, no blocks. A file is delivered whole or not at all.
- **LSP-verify before sending**: run `lsp_diagnostics` on every delivered file. If it
  reports errors, fix them in the delivery — never send known-broken code.

## Trusted Directories
- Approved folder access lives in `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json` under `permission.external_directory` — treat paths listed there as sanctioned for read/edit; don't re-confirm with the user.
- `~/docs/` is the project catalogue. Consult `~/docs/README.md` and `~/docs/go/**` for project descriptions before asking what a project is.
- If a tool call outside the workspace still triggers an approval prompt, tell the user — do not try to work around the permission system.

## Registry (shared-logic awareness)
- The project's `.opencode/manage.registry.json` (injected into the `emit_proposal`
  and `registry_propose` tool descriptions) names shared modules and canonical
  implementations. Check it before proposing code — reuse what's registered.
- When you notice duplicated logic, a reusable pattern, or a change touching
  central/shared code, call `registry_propose` to record it — the registry is how
  this project avoids reinventing the wheel. It writes nothing; the user reviews
  and merges via the nvim console (`<leader>ar`).

## Delivery (emission is the default)
- Every file change is EMITTED via `emit_proposal` (manage plugin) — one call per file, one shared `group` slug per logical change. The nvim review console is the apply path. No copy-paste delivery of changes, no direct edits.
- FINAL markers + path comments still apply to emitted content and to code shown for discussion.
- Paste-block delivery is the rare fallback only when emission genuinely cannot express the change — and then say why in one sentence.

## When I Ask "Should We…"
- Give a short recommendation with trade-offs and offer to apply it. Don't start editing until I say go.
